Let’s break down Fixed and Dynamic Partitioning — two classical Memory Management techniques used in operating systems to manage main memory.

🧠 1. Fixed Partitioning (Static Partitioning)
📘 Definition:
In fixed partitioning, the main memory is divided into a fixed number of partitions at system startup.
Each partition has a fixed size, and a process is loaded into one of these partitions.

⚙️ How It Works:
Memory is divided into partitions of equal or unequal size.

Each partition can contain only one process at a time.

A process is assigned to a partition that is large enough to accommodate it.

🧩 Example:
Assume main memory = 1 GB divided as:

Partition	Size	Status
P1	256 MB	Free
P2	256 MB	Free
P3	512 MB	Free
If a process of 200 MB arrives → it can fit into P1 or P2.
If a process of 400 MB arrives → it must go into P3.

✅ Advantages:
Simple to implement

Low overhead for OS (partitions known in advance)

Fast allocation (just pick a free partition)

❌ Disadvantages:
Internal Fragmentation
→ If process size < partition size, the unused space in that partition is wasted.
(e.g., process 200 MB in 256 MB partition → 56 MB wasted)

Limited number of processes
→ Number of partitions = maximum number of processes in memory.

Inefficient memory utilization
→ Small processes waste space, large ones might not fit.

⚡ 2. Dynamic Partitioning (Variable Partitioning)
📘 Definition:
In dynamic partitioning, memory is not pre-divided.
Partitions are created dynamically when processes are loaded — each partition exactly fits the process size.

⚙️ How It Works:
When a process arrives, it is allocated exactly the amount of memory it needs.

When it finishes, that memory is freed and can be reused.

Over time, free memory becomes scattered → external fragmentation may occur.

🧩 Example:
Assume 1024 MB total memory.

Process A → 200 MB
Remaining = 824 MB

Process B → 300 MB
Remaining = 524 MB

Process A finishes → 200 MB hole created
Free blocks: 200 MB + 524 MB (not contiguous)

If Process C = 250 MB → can’t fit in 200 MB hole, even though total free = 724 MB.

✅ Advantages:
No internal fragmentation (since memory fits process exactly)

Better memory utilization

Flexible — partitions created on demand

❌ Disadvantages:
External Fragmentation
→ Free memory scattered in small holes.

Compaction needed
→ To combine free blocks, OS must occasionally compact memory, which is slow.

More complex allocation algorithms
→ Requires strategies like:

First Fit — first hole large enough

Best Fit — smallest hole large enough

Worst Fit — largest available hole
----------------------------------- 
🔹 B. Non-Contiguous Memory Allocation
Processes do not need to occupy consecutive memory blocks.

1) Paging: Memory divided into fixed-size blocks:
Frame (physical memory)
Page (logical memory)
Eliminates external fragmentation but may cause internal fragmentation.

2) Segmentation: Memory divided based on logical divisions (function, array, data, etc.).Each segment has variable size.
Easier for programmer but leads to external fragmentation.

3) Paged Segmentation:Combines paging + segmentation for better efficiency.
